= Conclusion
The purpose of this study was to investigate how therapists reason about their agency and how it should be distributed across the main phases of exergame-based rehabilitation. The research questions were (1) How do therapists reason about their agency in relation to a human-AI system for exergame-based rehabilitation therapy?, and (2) How do therapists' preferences for agency distribution between themselves and a system (AI) vary across the main phases of exergame-based rehabilitation therapy (e.g., planning, etc)?. To answer the research questions, the study *took a* participatory Research through Design (RtD) approach by employing two main methods: (1) a contextual inquiry conducted through a case study (Brain Stimulation) including interviews and observation, and (2) individual participatory speculative co-design sessions. Eight participants in total, with an average of *X years* were involved in the study: five participated in the contextual inquiry and five in the co-design session. The concluding results from the affinity diagramming suggests .... *mention more details than in abstract?* While the research sample is small, the participants and methods were of qualitative nature, thereby reliably contributing to more-than-human design through the lens of therapists agency within AI-supported exergame-based rehabilitation systems'. We however suggest future researchers, product owners and students to continue to explore therapists agency through more dimensions within all four phases of therapy to accurately determine the design implications for a human-in-the-loop AI-supported computational system for exergame-based rehabilitation.
